  <data name="HomePage_HeroTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Live Photo Box</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_HeroSubtitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>— Make every memory feel alive like anime (o・v・o)</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_WelcomeTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Welcome to Live Photo Box</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_Intro1.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This is a professional yet easy-to-use Live Photo editing tool designed to help you easily break down the device ecosystem barriers between iOS and Android. It can losslessly convert Live Photos taken on your iPhone into formats natively supported by Google Photos, perfectly preserving the original video quality and dynamic frame rate, allowing your vivid memories to be perfectly presented on any platform.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_Intro2.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Beyond the Live Photos compatibility, I also discovered a significant drawback of Apple's design. To optimize front-facing camera performance, Live Photos uses horizontal storage for the video portion of the physical pixels and employs a built-in Rotation tag to determine the photo's orientation. However, Windows and other systems ignore the tag and directly render the raw pixels, causing the image to rotate. Prior to iOS 17.3, there was also an issue with stretched thumbnails. Apple attempted to add additional low-resolution thumbnails to ensure correct display, but these efforts were unsuccessful. The Thumbnail Fix feature was thus created, achieving lossless pixel-level reconstruction through underlying physical correction, completely resolving the persistent thumbnail compatibility problem on non-Apple devices.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_TipLabel.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Tip :</value>
  </data>
  <data name="HomePage_TipText.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Before using merge or split features, make sure you have backups of the original files. Data safety comes first.</value>
  </data>
